{"kind":"artifact","text":"The gs status reference now states the distinction in plain English, per ISO 24495-1. A new section, Two kinds of staleness, says that ordinary reasoning staleness means a basis under a record was retired so the reasoning moved while the record did not, and that it blocks nothing; and that a superseded world is narrower, means the replaced behaviour, and stops a merge. It then says what the default view does with each: counts ordinary staleness per lane and marks no row, keeps finished commitments out of the lists whether or not they are stale, and keeps retired state words and superseded-world notes on their own rows with their own counts. It names the escape hatches — --all prints a stale qualifier per commitment, --json is untouched, and the bounded summary keeps its per-row stale field. This satisfies condition 3. Tests passed: go test ./internal/docset/... -count=1 (all four documentation gates), and go test ./... -count=1.","body":{"branch":"request/staleness-presentation","commit":"921989f1c851cdcddae3dd7edf02e49f9db632cf","head":"921989f1c851cdcddae3dd7edf02e49f9db632cf","path":"docs/reference/gs/status.md"}}